%T A180205 490253312,4455645952,43468799232,445393216256,4783835680768,
%U A180205 54372476838912,653068724224000,8169310794943488,106298966614843392,1449278709531697152,20677932905765044224
%N A180205 Number of permutations of 0..(n-1) representable as consecutive sums of 2 adjacent elements of a sequence of n+1 nonnegative integers.
%H A180205 Carlo Corti, <a href="/A180205/b180205.txt">Table of n, a(n) for n = 1..28</a>
%H A180205 Carlo Corti, <a href="https://doi.org/10.5281/zenodo.24311714">Exact Enumeration of Adjacent-Sum Permutations: OEIS A180205 through n=28</a>, Zenodo, 2026.
%F A180205 From _Carlo Corti_, Nov 17 2026: (Start)
%F A180205 For a finite set S of positive integers, let k = |S| and let F(S) count the orderings (v(1),...,v(k)) of S for which x(j) >= 0 for 1 <= j <= k, where x(0) = 0 and x(j) = v(j) - x(j-1). Set F({}) = 1.
%F A180205 a(n) = Sum_{S subseteq U} F(S)*F(U \ S), where U = {1,...,n-1}, for n >= 1.
%F A180205 a(n+1) >= 2*a(n), and 2^(n-1) <= a(n) <= n!, for n >= 1.
%F A180205 a(n) == 0 (mod 2) for n > 1. (End)
%e A180205 For n = 4, F(S) = 1 for every proper subset S of {1,2,3}, while F({1,2,3}) = 3. Thus a(4) = 6 + 2*3 = 12. - _Carlo Corti_, Nov 17 2026
%Y A180205 Cf. A180206, A180207, A180208, A180209, A180210, A180211, A180212.
%K A180205 nonn,changed
